{*
	A Latte tag standing inside CSS or JavaScript. Everything below is valid
	Latte 2.11 and valid output, and nothing here may be underlined.

	The plugin gives the IDE an HTML view of the template so that HTML, CSS and
	JavaScript are highlighted and checked. Building that view drops every Latte
	tag out of the text, so what CSS and JavaScript are handed is the template
	with holes in it - and a hole in the wrong place is not something either of
	them can parse.

	Two of these are reported today and are kept here so that the day they stop
	being reported is visible. Both are in SandboxTemplatesAreQuietTest under
	KNOWN_FALSE_POSITIVES; the rest of the file is quiet and has to stay so.
*}

{varType string $colour}
{varType string $property}
{varType int $width}
{varType string $name}
{varType int $value}

{* A tag standing for a whole value, with and without a unit. Both are quiet. *}
<div style="background-color: {$colour}">whole value</div>
<div style="width: {$width}px">value with a unit</div>

{* A tag standing for the property name. Quiet as well. *}
<div style="{$property}: red">property name</div>

{*
	A tag standing for the digits of a hex colour, which is how a template
	writes one it was given without the hash. Reported: the hash is left with
	nothing after it and CSS asks for a term.
*}
<div style="background-color: #{$colour}">hash before the tag</div>

<script>
	/* A tag standing for a value, and for a whole statement. Both quiet. */
	var fromAValue = {$value};
	{$name|noescape}

	/*
		A tag standing for the name of a variable, which is how a template names
		one per item of a loop. Reported: what is left is "var  = 1" and
		JavaScript reads the brace that opens the tag as a binding of its own.
	*/
	var {$name|noescape} = 1;
</script>
